## Architectural versus informational ceiling

[RUN] Widening only the three hidden layers increased sealed LOAO ordering by `0.0517` and
top-1 by `0.0345`, but reduced level `R²` by another `9.0514`. LOWO did not improve. The
linear and flexible models both transferred substantially more ordering signal than the
sealed head. This quantifies a material architectural/optimization penalty.

[RUN] The best observed level fit of any tested function of the same Q2 inputs was the
high-capacity model's LOAO `R² = 0.0505` and LOWO `0.0166`; both intervals include zero.
Even after giving that model the absorbing transform exactly, its top-1 was only `0.3000`
LOAO and `0.3085` LOWO. Its LOAO ordering decomposed into `0.8090` on the 10.43% of
non-tied pairs crossing lost counts and `0.6310` within a lost-count class. Thus it learned
more than the modal floor, but most continuous level variance and reliable argmax choice
remain unavailable to the tested same-input functions.

[INFERRED] The useful ceiling is therefore predominantly informational: greater capacity
does not produce persuasive level or top-1 prediction. The sealed head also has a separate,
important architectural/optimization problem because it leaves recoverable ordering signal
on the table. The all-nonzero BASE targets and the reference-zero training gauge are a
specific C2 level mismatch, but the weak best flexible level fit shows that this mismatch
is not the whole explanation.

